    <h1 class="fade-up">An artist's garden: <em>atmospheric</em> planting around the studio</h1>
    <p class="lead fade-up d2">Designing a garden for an artist&#x27;s studio: atmospheric, painterly planting with soft colour, movement and light that changes through the day and the seasons.</p>
    <div class="hero__actions fade-up d3"><a class="btn" href="/contact.html">Discuss your garden <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></div>
  </div></div>
</section>
<section class="section"><div class="wrap prose-grid">
  <article class="prose reveal"><p class="lead">Artists notice everything: the way light falls across a wall at four o'clock, the colour of a seedhead in November, the movement of grass in wind. A garden for an artist, or around a studio, should give them something to look at. Above all, it should be <strong>atmospheric</strong>.</p>
<h2>Planting like a painter</h2>
<p>Atmospheric planting is about mood rather than display. It uses a limited palette of colours repeated through the garden, plants with soft, see-through forms, and a lot of texture. The result looks less like a border and more like a painting: layered, hazy and changing all the time.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>See-through plants</strong> such as Verbena bonariensis, thalictrum, sanguisorba and grasses veil the view and add depth.</li>
<li><strong>A restrained palette</strong> of, for example, plum, apricot and silver, or white and green, repeated so the eye travels.</li>
<li><strong>Flowers that age well:</strong> dahlias, roses and hydrangeas whose faded tones are as beautiful as the fresh ones.</li>
</ul>
<figure><div class="reveal-img"><img src="/assets/img/dahlia-pink.webp" alt="Pink pompon dahlia in soft focus with yellow flowers behind" width="1400" height="936" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Dahlias: generous, painterly and in flower from midsummer until the first frost.</figcaption></figure>
<h2>Light and the studio</h2>
<p>Studios are often lit from the north, and the view from the studio window matters. We place planting so that it catches low sun from behind (grasses and seedheads glow when backlit) and keep the view from the working space calm, with bright colour saved for areas seen on the way in and out.</p>
<div class="pull">A garden around a studio should be something to look at, not something to look after.</div>
<h2>Gardens that change</h2>
<p>An artist's garden should never be finished. We often leave room in the planting plan for self-seeders such as poppies, foxgloves and verbascum, which move around the garden from year to year and keep it unpredictable. Seedheads are left standing through winter for their silhouettes and for the birds.</p>
<div class="duo"><div class="reveal-img"><img src="/assets/img/dahlias.webp" srcset="/assets/img/dahlias-800.webp 800w, /assets/img/dahlias.webp 1600w" sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 50vw" alt="Coral, peach and cream dahlias in full bloom" width="1600" height="1067" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div><div class="reveal-img"><img src="/assets/img/meadow-path.webp" srcset="/assets/img/meadow-path-800.webp 800w, /assets/img/meadow-path.webp 1600w" sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 50vw" alt="Mown path running through long meadow grasses" width="1600" height="1067" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div></div><h2>Practical studio gardens</h2>
<p>Studios need access for materials, somewhere to work outside and somewhere to wash brushes and tools. We design those practical spaces into the garden so they are attractive in their own right, rather than an afterthought.</p></article>
  <aside class="aside">
    <div class="aside-card reveal"><h3>At a glance</h3><dl><div><dt>Typical size</dt><dd>50 to 500 m²</dd></div><div><dt>Best for</dt><dd>Studios, creative homes</dd></div><div><dt>Season of interest</dt><dd>Late spring to winter</dd></div><div><dt>Signature plants</dt><dd><div class="palette"><span>Dahlias</span><span>Roses</span><span>Cosmos</span><span>Verbena bonariensis</span><span>Deschampsia</span><span>Thalictrum</span></div></dd></div></dl></div>
